.TH CHARBUF_PRED_LOOKAHEAD 3 "2023-04-01" "1.0" "lib/charbuf/charbuf_pred_lookahead"
.SH NAME
charbuf_pred_lookahead \- Look ahead for characters in a character buffer based on a predicate
.SH SYNOPSIS
#include <lib/charbuf.h>
#include <lib/stralloc.h>

ssize_t charbuf_pred_lookahead(charbuf *b, int (*pred)(int, size_t, void*), void *ptr);
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBcharbuf_pred_lookahead\fR function looks ahead at characters in the character buffer \fBb\fR and counts the number of characters that satisfy a predicate function \fBpred\fR. The predicate function is called for each character in the buffer, and if it returns non-zero, the character is counted. The function continues to look ahead until the predicate returns zero or there are no more characters to look ahead.

This function is useful for counting the number of characters in a buffer that satisfy a certain condition without consuming them.
.SH ARGUMENTS
\fBb\fR
The character buffer to look ahead in.
\fBpred\fR
A predicate function that determines whether a character should be counted.
\fBptr\fR
A pointer to data that will be passed to the predicate function.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The number of characters that satisfy the predicate.
.SH EXAMPLE
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBcharbuf_pred_lookahead\fR function:
.nf
#include <lib/charbuf.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int is_alpha(int c, size_t len, void *ptr) {
    (void)ptr; // Unused
    return (c >= 'a' && c <= 'z') || (c >= 'A' && c <= 'Z');
}

int main() {
    charbuf b;
    charbuf_init(&b);
    charbuf_append(&b, "Hello, world!");

    size_t count = charbuf_pred_lookahead(&b, is_alpha, NULL);

    printf("Number of alphabetic characters: %zu\n", count);

    charbuf_free(&b);

    return 0;
}
.ne
.fi
